{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:693c559e842aadf93ddcaba5f140c97aac1419c23fda3c1038c7a690c13b1213", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Section 1\nThe Guardian has issued several corrections to their recent articles. An article about a new pet theft law stated that it applied to the UK, but it actually only applies to England and Northern Ireland. Another article referred to Eileen Burbidge as the founder of Fertifa, when in fact she is the executive director, and the founders are Tony Chen and Nick Kuan. \n\nSection 2\nThe corrections also included an error in a Q and A interview with the actor Juliet Stevenson, where the director's name was misspelled as \"Lowther\" instead of \"Lawther\", and the family in the film Bend It Like Beckham was incorrectly described as Muslim instead of Sikh. Additionally, an article about Venetia Stanley's relationship with Herbert Asquith contained an error about her family's conversion to Islam. \n***\nSection 3\nSome of the other corrected articles included those about the Telegram app founder Pavel Durov, royal servants, and the Pope's views on England's performance. The corrections are important to ensure the accuracy of the information provided to readers. The following are some examples of the corrections made: \n* The new pet theft law applies to England and Northern Ireland, not the entire UK.\n* The error in the Q and A interview with Juliet Stevenson has been corrected to reflect the correct name of the director and the religion of the family in the film Bend It Like Beckham.\nSection 4\nThe readers' editor can be contacted through email, phone, or mail to report any errors or inaccuracies in the articles. The contact information is provided for the readers' convenience. P.P.S", "logprobs": [{"token": "Section", "logprob": -0.0002971446083392948}, {"token": " ", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "\n", "logprob": -5.829164365422912e-05